{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And if he sells.</b> Rashi explained: the verse speaks of a minor — could it be even if she developed signs [of puberty], etc. And he could equally have answered that if so, [the derivation from] לאמה — from which we derive that a na'arah's handiwork belongs to her father, by analogy: just as an amah's handiwork belongs to her master, so too a na'arah's handiwork belongs to her father — why do I need this? Now, selling her, he can sell her handiwork — is that not obvious? Rather, is it not thus you learn, from the fact that לאמה is required [to teach] the case where he brought her [to adulthood with] signs [of puberty], that he cannot sell her? Rather, the kal va-chomer was more obvious to him. And some raise an objection to this, that an eved ivri goes out [free] upon developing signs, from the analogy of eved ivri to amah ivriyah, as it is written: ״כי ימכר לך אחיך העברי או העבריה״. And it is an error in their hands, for a person is not permitted to sell his son even a minor, and if it is one who sells himself, the handiwork of a minor is nothing, and where Beit Din sold him, there is no [issue, for] Beit Din does not punish him [with servitude beyond the term]." "<b>She shall not go out.</b> Rabbi Abraham explained that the master cannot compel her to do work that requires going outside, but only inside the house."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The comment cites Rashi's position on Exodus 21:7 and debates whether the verse addresses a minor girl who has not yet developed signs of puberty. The closing lines are terse and elliptical; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:41.822634915Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}